It depends on the zone's `rate` and whether `/health` is excluded with its own `location` block.

nginx applies `limit_req` per the `rate` you set, e.g. `limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=1r/s;` allows one request per second per key, with bursts only if you configure `burst=`. A request every 5 seconds (0.2 r/s) passes any zone rated 1 r/s or higher. It gets blocked only if:

- the zone rate is below one request per 5 seconds (rare; e.g. `rate=1r/m`),
- `burst=0` and the request arrives before the leaky-bucket slot refills,
- or `/health` sits under the same `location` as the limited path with no bypass.

Common pattern: exempt health checks so monitors never get throttled:

```
location /health {
    access_log off;
    limit_req off;
    proxy_pass http://app;
}
```

nginx matches one `location` block per request, so a dedicated `/health` block with `limit_req off` keeps the monitor out of the zone entirely.

